'''''Hachinawa''''' — ''hachin'' "centre" + ''awa'' "realm", "the middle realm" — is the state of the [[Hachinawa|Hahkina]] people, occupying the central plain of [[Sinchew]] on the western coast of [[Lurandia]]. It is the oldest and largest polity of the region, and the only one whose wealth rested on grain agriculture rather than on fishing, forest produce or pastoralism.

By the time the first Zong expedition sighted its coast in 1458, Hachinawa had been a state for some eight centuries, possessed a logographic script, a solar calendar and a system of grain accounting, and governed a centralised core on the plain together with a ring of tributary neighbours in the surrounding uplands. Its kings were elected by the council of the realm from the members of the royal house — an arrangement that contact with [[Zong]] would begin to change.

== Plain ==

The territory of Hachinawa comprises about 328,196 km², the second largest of the nine peoples of Sinchew, and is the most uniform of them. Some seventy-nine per cent of it lies in a single elevation band between 260 and 430 metres, a gently rolling plain without mountain barriers or internal divisions of terrain.

The climate is the driest and most seasonal in Sinchew: a tropical savanna regime over some sixty per cent of the territory with a humid subtropical regime over thirty, and a median annual rainfall of about 1,425 mm. This is a region of distinct wet and dry seasons rather than of continuous rain, and it is the only climate in Sinchew suited to cereals.

The plain is drained by a dense river network and watered by canals. It produced a grain surplus, and the surplus was the foundation of the state: a concentrated and transportable harvest is what funds taxation, a standing force, a priesthood, monumental building and a class of officials who keep records.

== Origins of the state ==

=== First towns ===

Irrigation agriculture is attested on the plain from about 350 CE, with canal-fed cultivation of maize and the appearance of the first walled settlements. The towns of this period were small and independent, each with its own granary and its own line of canals, and there is no evidence of a single authority over more than a day's march.

=== Unification ===

The plain was unified under a single king in about 640 CE. The tradition credits this to [[Kayahik]], who is said to have taken the plain not by conquering each town but by holding the canal heads, on which every town downstream depended. Whether or not the account is accurate, it describes the right mechanism: in an irrigation society, control of the water is control of everything below it.

Kayahik established the capital at [[Hachintala]], at the centre of the plain on a river, where it has remained.

The unification is the founding event of Hachinawa, and the state's name — "the middle realm" — expresses it. The kingdom is not named for its people, its dynasty or its capital but for its ''position'': the centre, with everything else arranged around it.

=== Calendar and the script ===

The script and the calendar appeared within two generations of unification, about 700 CE, and they appeared together.

The calendar is solar, divided according to the agricultural year, and its earliest function was to fix the dates of sowing, irrigation and harvest. The script is logographic, with a numeral system of its own, and its earliest surviving use is not literary but administrative: the recording of grain — quantities received, quantities stored, quantities issued — and the dates on which they moved.

Writing in Hachinawa therefore begins as accounting. The monuments and the literature come later, and the oldest inscriptions are granary records on stone: what came in, what went out, and when.

== Governance ==

=== King ===

The ruler of Hachinawa bears the title hik, rendered in Zong as king. He is not a god and is not worshipped, but his office is the centre of the state: he commands the army, opens and closes the agricultural year by the calendar, and receives the tribute of the uplands.

Kings take a regnal name on accession, built from the roots of the state's own vocabulary — ''hachin'' (centre), ''tsula'' (lord), ''kaya'' (plain), ''hina'' (river), ''hacha'' (grain), ''sho'' (gold). His birth name is not used after his accession and is not recorded in the official lists. [[Tsulhachi]], king at the time of the [[Treaty of Anping]], takes his name from ''tsula'' and ''hachin'' — "lord of the centre" — which is what a king of the middle realm would wish to be called.

=== Nawe ===

The nawe is the council of the realm. It comprises the great landholding houses of the plain, the senior officials of the capital, and the heads of the principal priestly lineages.

Before contact with Zong, the king was elected by the nawe from the members of the royal house. The succession was not hereditary by primogeniture. On the death of a king, the council chose among the qualified men of the house — in practice the ablest adult, with age, achievement and the support of the great houses all weighing in the choice. The office was therefore within one family but not within one line, and a king's son had no automatic claim to follow him.

=== Tributaries ===

Beyond the plain, the uplands are lower in productivity and depend on the plain for grain. The peoples of those uplands — [[Uwisa]] and [[Nahola]] to the south and east, and [[Yelani]] in the highlands — were made tributary to Hachinawa over the centuries following unification.

The relationship was indirect. The tributary peoples kept their own rulers and their own laws, and Hachinawa appointed no governors among them. What was required was tribute — gold, together with labour, troops and raw materials — and the acknowledgment of the Hachina king's precedence. The submission of the uplands was obtained less by conquest than by the dependence that the grain trade created, and it fluctuated: a strong king collected regularly, a weak one collected in name.

Hachinawa is therefore a centralised state at home and an empire of clients abroad. It governs the plain directly, through districts and appointed officials taxed in grain, and governs the mountains hardly at all.

== Language and writing ==

{{Main|Hachina language|Scripts of Hachinawa}}

The language of Hachinawa is [[Hachina language|Hachina]], the larger of the two languages of the Hachina family, the other being [[Hatako]]. It is not related to any of the other language families of Sinchew, and no relationship to the [[Nahola languages|Nahola]], [[Uwisa language|Uwisa]] or [[Yelani language|Yelani]] families has been demonstrated.

Hachina was the language of the court, of the administration of the plain and of the temple schools. It was not the language of the empire's tributaries, who kept their own languages and their own rulers with them; the empire's correspondence with its clients was conducted in Hachina, and Hachina was therefore the language in which the empire addressed peoples who did not speak it.

Hachina was written from about 700 CE in a logographic script of its own, called [[Scripts of Hachinawa|Dalewa]] - ''dale'', "speech", and ''awa'', "realm", "the realm of speech". The name carries the same construction as the state's own name, and names the script for what it is rather than for its inventor or its material.

''Dalewa'' was taught in the temple establishments from its invention, together with the calendar and the numerals, and the officials who measured the grain and the priests who kept the calendar were trained in a single curriculum of which the script was part. For seven centuries its ordinary medium was stone: the script was cut into monuments, boundary stones, granary walls and temples, and the record it produced was public, durable and brief.

In the sixteenth century the [[Zong]] script was adopted by decree of the king in place of ''Dalewa'', and the change, with what followed from it, is treated in the [[History of Hachinawa (1538-1606)|following period]].

== Religion ==

Hachinawa followed a religion with a single supreme being and a body of subordinate powers, and its central term is the same word as the name of the state.

=== Supreme being ===

The supreme being is Hachin — "the Centre". It is not a god that possesses a centre but the centre itself: the point about which the world is ordered and from which order proceeds. The faith is monotheistic in its structure, there being one source of order and no rival to it, but the world is not administered by that being directly, and the powers that act within it are honoured in their own right.

Two of these are named. Kayawa — from ''kaya'', "plain", and ''awa'', "realm" — is the power of the earth, and is the recipient of the principal cult: the one addressed at sowing and at harvest. Kuhina — from ''ku'', "water", and ''hina'', "river" — is the power of the waters, and is invoked in the maintenance of the canals and at the opening of the irrigation season. Both are understood as agents of the Centre rather than as gods in their own right, and neither is worshipped in opposition to it.

The naming of the state follows from the theology. Hachinawa, "the realm of the centre", is not a claim about geography but about the presence of the Centre within the realm; the ethnonym Hachina, "the central people", is a claim about the being they worship rather than about their position on a map. Every royal throne-name built on ''hachin'' — Tsulhachi, "lord of the centre" — names the king as the agent of the supreme being.

=== King and the calendar ===

The king is not divine and is not worshipped. He is the only person who may open the agricultural year, and the fixing of the dates of sowing, irrigation and harvest is his function.

This is the substance of his office. The religion holds that the order of the world is expressed in the calendar, and the calendar is proclaimed by the king; a state that sows on the right day prospers and one that sows on the wrong day starves. He neither intercedes with the Centre on behalf of his people nor embodies it. He performs the order.

=== Priesthood ===

The priesthood is hereditary. The priestly lineages hold their office by descent, and the senior of them supplies the high priest, who sits in the [[History of Hachinawa|nawe]] by right rather than by appointment and ranks with the great landholding houses rather than above them.

The priests keep the calendar and the records of the cult, and they staff the temple complexes. Those complexes serve several purposes at once: they are places of worship, of record, and of instruction. The schools in which officials were trained in the script, the numerals and the calendar were attached to them, so that the temple was also the archive and the school.

=== Death and the ancestors ===

The dead are gathered to their ancestors, and the ancestors remain at the centre.

There is no judgement and no separate paradise in the religion. What follows death is continuity with the house to which the dead belonged, and the dead of the royal and priestly lineages are kept at or near [[Hachintala]], the capital. Tombs are therefore most numerous among the royal and priestly lineages, and the dead of those lineages remain part of the state.

=== Relation to Tianism ===

The religion of Hachinawa and [[Tianism]], the faith of [[Zong]], share a structure. Both hold a single supreme source of order; both hold that the world is ordered by it; and in both the ruler is the agent of that order rather than a god in himself. The vocabulary differs and the calendars are not the same, but the shape of the two beliefs is close enough that neither people had to treat the other as religiously alien.

This is the ground of the understanding between Hachinawa and the Zong. Neither side was asked to abandon a pantheon, because neither had one; neither was asked to accept a rival to its supreme being, because each understood the other to be worshipping under a single order. The Zong met a people who already held that authority descended from a heavenly order, and the Hachina met a foreign power that was not, in religious terms, a category error. The [[Treaty of Anping]] was negotiated between two states whose rulers were, in their own theologies, the agents of one god each.

