== Contact with Zong ==

The Zong first sighted the Hachina coast in 1458, during the [[History of Sinchew (1474-1538)|first Gulf expedition]]. The Zong colonial administration named the people twice: Hâ-pang (遐邦), "the distant state", which acknowledged a state while declining to repeat its claim to be the centre; and later Hahkina (哈其那), the people's own name, rendered phonetically — a change of register that marks the point at which the Zong stopped describing Hachinawa on a map and began negotiating with it.

Relations remained distant until the 1530s, when the colonial administration sought an ally against the peoples of the northern coast. The [[Treaty of Anping]] of 1538 recognised the Hachina king as a sovereign and provided for the joint campaign that became the [[First Mahik War]].

The elective succession became a problem in these years. The Zong dealt with Tsulhachi as a sovereign and expected his son to follow him; the Zong's treaty of 1538, its arms supply arrangement, and its later dealings all assume a line of succession. Whether the change came about through Hachina adoption of the foreign expectation or through the advantage it gave a faction within the nawe, the practice of election from the royal house narrowed after contact, and the throne began to pass from father to son.

